issue.body:
If I do something like this:
```
let m = openpgp.message.readArmored(armored_m);

Promise.all([m.decrypt(k1), m.decrypt(k2)]).then(results => {
  console.log(results[0]);
  console.log(results[1]);
})
```
One of the results will always fail, because the first successful result will wipe data packets for the second one (memory optimization?).

Knowing this, I can refactor my code to account for it. I just feel like I can't be the only person trying to follow a pattern where you may attempt to decrypt the same message object several times with different parameters, later checking which one worked.

Solutions:

**Option 1)** respond with the same success result on subsequent decrypt attempts just like PrivateKey.decrypt() does

**Option 2)** Set a flag/parameter on the message object during first successful decrypt just before wiping the data packets. Watch this flag during subsequent attempts, and raise a specific error: "Encrypted data packets were removed during previous successful decrypt attempt"

**Option 3)** Consider it a feature and forget about it. Maybe a warning in a "known limitations" somewhere.

Option 1 is consistent with how PrivateKey.decrypt() behaves. Option 2 is a bit stricter, because Option 1 allows you to supply it a wrong private key on second attempt and it will look like that wrong key has decrypted the message, possibly causing confusion.

