comment.body:
I mean when the message originates at the consumer.

    topic1 -> consumer -> processor -> topic2

Transactions work when a new message arrives at the consumer; the consumer starts the transaction, calls the stream listener, which sends to the transacted producer; finally, the consumer sends the topic offset to the transaction then commits the transaction.

In your case, you are sending the message back to yourself so the producer needs to start the transaction. The consumer will start a different transaction.

See my referenced pull request for how I fixed the binder to support producer-initiated transactions.

Until that is merged, you can use a `KafkaTemplate` to send a transactional message, either by calling it within a `TransactionTemplate.execute()` similar to what I did in the PR, or like this...

```java
template.executeInTransaction(t -> {
	t.sendDefault(0, 0, "foo");
	return null;
});
 ```

